    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d595">

                <pb facs="0719" n="713" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg713"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/988a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in the Soviet
                        Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-07-08T04:00:00-04:00">July 8, 1942—4
                            a.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>188. From McCabe for Faymonville. Mr. Hopkins has directed Brigadier General S.
                    P. Spalding to undertake a survey of the supply route to Russia by way of the
                    Persian Gulf for the purpose of ascertaining whether everything possible is
                    being done to improve transport facilities and to make certain that material to
                    be delivered under the second Protocol will be forwarded as promptly and as
                    efficiently as possible. General Spalding has been instructed to make
                    recommendations for improvement and to make full report after return to United
                    States. It is desirable that General Spalding confer in Iran with Soviet
                    authorities there and inspect those portions of the route <pb facs="0720"
                        n="714" xml:id="pg714"/> under Soviet control, including port, road and rail
                    facilities, in order that he will be able to make certain that the project is
                    synchronized in all its phases and be in a position to recommend the furnishing
                    by the United States of necessary facilities and equipment.</p>
                <p>It would be most helpful if you could arrange to meet General Spalding at some
                    convenient location, possibly at Tehran, in order that you may be mutually
                    informed regarding the situation. We are requesting General Belyaev’s
                    cooperation in facilitating the mission of General Spalding. [McCabe.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
